[31;1m[0m
[31;1mThe last cutoff without an outcome is emitted as `pending_outcome`: that is the current week's list (five securities per forecaster), archived in `data/raw` with a real timestamp.[0m
[31;1m[0m
[31;1mThe daily capture runs as a Windows scheduled task since 9 Sep 2026 (18:30 local time) via `scripts/register_daily_capture.ps1`; remove it with `-Eliminar`.[0m
[31;1m[0m
[31;1m**Weekly prospective cycle** (phase 3): `scripts/weekly_prospective.ps1`, registered by `scripts/register_weekly_prospective.ps1` as the Windows task "taiwan-ia-lab ciclo semanal" (Sundays 08:00 local Eastern time = 20:00 Taipei in summer, 21:00 in winter; in both cases after the 18:00 cutoff and before the Monday 08:30 deadline). It fetches the missing official sessions, runs the three scenarios up to the Taipei date with the original archive labels (`--archive-label`, so weeks already archived are reused byte-for-byte and keep their first ingestion time), rebuilds the report headers, exports the site and publishes it. A list counts as a *prediction* on the site only when, for every forecaster, the first archived ingestion of the exact bytes evaluated (sha256 recorded in the run) is intact, was written by the system clock and is at or before the `deadline_at` that prediction declares; the archived prediction is well formed (`selected` with a non-empty ranking of unique securities and strict-integer ranks 1..n in array order, or an empty abstention) and declares the week's packet hash and cutoff; the displayed status, list, symbols and names equal the archived ranking and the names carried by the archived packet; the archived packet deserialises under the lab contract, its recomputed logical hash matches the week, the file and the archive record, and every capture it references is intact, system-clocked and ingested before the cutoff (integrity is re-checked on every classification; the archive index is re-read atomically whenever its bytes change and one classification sees exactly one generation of it; no verdict is cached; evidence must live inside `data/raw` (no absolute paths, `..` or links that leave it); the first copy that counts is the first *intact* one, and only then is its clock judged); and every selected security has, in the master snapshot archived by that run and re-serialised for every week, verified unchanged when the week's predictions have been archived (each archived prediction cites that snapshot's sha256; its first intact system-clocked ingestion must be at or before both the deadline and the first ingestion of every prediction), a segment valid at the cutoff *as known at the cutoff* (rows recorded later do not count) whose symbol is the archived ticker; every price series in the archived packet must declare exactly one security, valid at the cutoff, and carry its symbol. Records with wrong types in the archive index, the prediction, the packet or the master snapshot (whose rows must all be well-formed segments) are rejected by typed checks plus the lab's own contract validators, and any other exception closes only the stage where it happens (reported as `classification_error:<type>`, with the evidence already verified kept). On the emission side, a corrupt or missing archived packet, a corrupt or mistyped archive index, a missing capture or a master that changed during emission leaves that week recorded as `invalid:archive` (no complete list, no evaluation; whatever was archived before the failure stays traceable) while inherited baskets are still managed (rights, exit retries, valuation) and the run continues; the reports and the site show that state with its cause; missing or corrupt master and prediction copies are replaced by another intact copy or re-archived before anything cites them (a second daily task, `scripts/register_daily_quotes_fetch.ps1`, captures the per-date quotes every evening so that Friday's session is archived before the cutoff). The packet is still built in historical mode and there is no external timestamp; the site says both.[0m
